ate data processing in cases of violations, and it provides guidance to controllers and processors as well as to data subjects regarding their rights and remedies.\n\nThe agency’s governance structure includes a Conselho Diretor and a Diretor-Presidente, with functional, technical, decisional, administrative, and financial autonomy. It operates as an autonomous autarchy, with its own budget and patrimony to support regulatory activities. Its funding may derive from budget allocations, donations and legacies, sale or lease of assets, and investment income. In some descriptions, ANPD is portrayed as historically linked to the Presidency or the Casa Civil, reflecting its evolution toward full institutional autonomy; regardless, it is consistently presented as Brazil’s regulatory authority for data protection governance.\n\nANPD also pursues proactive maturity in privacy governance, developing and disseminating RIPD (Relatórios de Impacto à Proteção de Dados) requirements, governance guidelines for the public sector, and collaboration on international cooperation. It articulates two strategic pathways—Caminho A (Ecosystem and international cooperation) and Caminho B (Comercialização Técnica)—to reflect its broader governance role, ecosystem engagement, and potential monetization of technical expertise via partnerships and services. Overall, ANPD coordinates LGPD enforcement, guidance, compliance supervision, and governance, while engaging with national and international partners to promote data protection and privacy across Brazil."}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 3746, "completion_tokens": 2291, "total_tokens": 6037, "compute_duration_seconds": 12.72264575958252, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}